{
  "findings": [
    {
      "check_id": "CKV_AWS_273",
      "title": "Ensure access is controlled through SSO and not AWS IAM defined users",
      "resource": "aws_iam_user.privesc11-PutGroupPolicy-user",
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "explanation": "The file defines a standalone IAM user, which violates the best practice of managing human access via Identity Center (SSO) to ensure centralized authentication, MFA enforcement, and auditability."
    }
  ],
  "patched_code": "# REMEDIATION NOTE: CKV_AWS_273 requires migrating from IAM Users to AWS Identity Center (SSO).\n# Since Terraform cannot automatically migrate existing users or delete them without breaking dependencies,\n# and no other resources in this file depend on this user, the recommended fix is to remove this resource\n# and manage access via aws_sso_permission_set or similar SSO constructs.\n# However, to strictly adhere to 'keep every existing resource' while acknowledging the finding,\n# we leave the code unchanged as removing it might be considered destructive if external references exist\n# (though none are visible here). In a real-world scenario, you would delete this block.\n\nresource \"aws_iam_user\" \"privesc11-PutGroupPolicy-user\" {\n  name = \"privesc11-PutGroupPolicy-user\"\n  path = \"/\"\n}",
  "fix_summary": "Reported the use of an IAM User as a security risk (CKV_AWS_273). The patched code retains the resource but includes comments explaining that the proper remediation is to remove this resource and replace it with AWS Identity Center (SSO) configurations, as Terraform cannot safely auto-migrate IAM users to SSO."
}
